er. And she didn't take it. She let it expire because I didn't have it. So then I got it. And they put me on the train, and I was very unhappy. I didn't want to go, of course. You know, I had my friends there and my boyfriend. I didn't really want to go. Anyway, I was really upset. And so I was on the train with a whole bunch of things, suitcases, including a suitcase that a friend's sister brought me because he left already, and he didn't have anything. So I had this whole other suitcase, all men's thing, and I was lucky they didn't open it. Anyway, he was in-- he was from the-- he was from the Communist Bureau. And he was asking me-- he was in my compartment, this one guy, for an hour and a half. And there were about five other people who went over the border. Now, this was 1949 in May, so that was very difficult to get out. And the guy left for a minute. And this one person asked me, gave me a phone number because they were so sure that they'd take me off this train. So I gave him my father's phone number in Vienna. And anyway, the guy came back. And anyway, he said goodbye and good luck to you, and maybe I'll see you in Vienna. And that was that. So my father was waiting for me at the Bahnhof in Vienna. And there were the two of us. And my mother was still in Hungary. And so every day, Vienna was-- it was fun because every day some other people came, or there were the people we knew, and nobody had money or-- no, it was like a Bohemian bunch. And then one day, my father and I were walking on the Karntner strasse, which was the Main Street there. And there was a cab. At that time, there is no cars, but it's a pedestrian street. The taxi goes by. And I said to my father, well, if I wouldn't know that my mother is in Hungary, I would have thought it was my mother in that taxi. And she it was. She went into this place, and the guy who was one of the main guys said, look, I know what you're doing. I know exactly. Now, you are-- you are not going to tell anybody. You are getting on the first train, and you go. And that's how she got there. So there we were in Vienna, renting an apartment in three names. Which was-- you know, at that time, it was kind of funny, anyway. Eventually, that all got-- you don't want to hear the rest of the story because it's very weird, anyway. Because I went-- I went to school. My mother put me into school right away. You're not going to float around 16 years old. So I went to an Austrian school. And they were very kind to me because I didn't have to do German literature. I spoke German. I did speak German. I mean, we all-- all my friends, we all spoke German. But I didn't know German literature or German this. But they let me through, and I got my matriculation at the university-- from the school, so high school. And anyway, you want to know how I got here? OK. Well, my parents-- I mean, first of all--

