INT: Why?

GR: We wanted to go. Well, we had to go somewhere. We wanted to go to the States, but the States had a quota. So we couldn't go. Most of my parents had siblings in the states, but we couldn't. So Canada was a doable thing. And I'm going back. And so we went to Salzburg because the Canadian consulate was there. And unknown to us, my father had a spot on his lung. And they wouldn't let us in to Canada because-- he wasn't sick, but he had from-- probably from the typhus and everything else, he had this spot on his lung. So my father, who was in the fur business originally, and his major suppliers were in Leipzig, Germany. They all moved because that became East Germany. And then they went over to Frankfurt. So my father said, I have to do something. So he went over to Frankfurt, and he had renewed acquaintances with a lot of his suppliers. And they told him that, once he goes to Munich and opens an office there, they're going to supply him with fur, and he can do wholesale fur. So that's-- so they were-- so they were in Munich in the meantime. So my mother came from Munich, and we met in-- on the-- she wrote to this-- somebody wrote to this guy at the hotel management school. And they said they couldn't possibly take me because I wasn't 18, and 18-- you have to be exactly 18 years or older to go there because it's like it's a university. Anyway, not-- my mother didn't pay too much attention to that. She rushed me a letter. "Meet me at the station in Lausanne" at such and such. So we meet at the station. We go there. And I know-- I know this director's name was Dr. Schweitzer. And my mother could speak German with him. And he told-- she told him the whole story, that really I should really be here. And I speak all the languages, and we are refugees. And so anyway, I was taken to the school. So I went there for three years, and, you know, and I had a diploma there. And I never-- well, in the meantime, summer holidays, I did work in Swiss hotels, in really good ones. But the problem with women and hotels was a bummer. Because they didn't give you any good jobs. They give you jobs at night or weekends or stuff like that. So I arrived back to Munich, and they just opened up the Air France office. Or they were going to open the Air France office. Perfect. My French was perfect. And I could speak. Anyway, the guy's name was Monsieur and he hired me at the spot. But you have to go to Berlin, because they opened the office there. So I was there for a year and a half. And I had a wonderful time. But I didn't like to be in Germany. And I'm not-- to this day, I'm not a hysterical person. I was sitting on a streetcar. And I saw all the men in German uniforms in front of me, and I just had to get off. And I had an uncle, my mother's twin brother, who lived in New York and moved to California in the meantime. And he sent me a visitor's visa. So my mother said, you know, she said she's going to jump off the 44th floor if I go. You know, the whole bit, Yiddishe Mama. Anyway, but eventually, she thought it was a good idea to go for a visit. So it was very sad. Anyway, I arrived to New York. And I loved it. I said, I never want to go anywhere else. I mean, this is the continent I want to live on.

INT: Why?

GR: So I was in New York for a while. And then my uncle-- I had-- I have an aunt there who I loved, my father's sister, who I looked exactly alike. And my uncle moved back to California in the meantime. And so anyway, he was carrying on that I should come to California. I loved New York, but I did move to California. So I ended up in California. I went-- don't ask. I mean, I didn't like my aunt, my uncle's wife. And at that time, I went-- I was all of 21. I went to the Social Security office. I got myself a Social Security card. I rented an apartment. I went downtown LA. I got myself a job at a furrier to be a model and be public relations or something. So I made, like, $200 a month. $50 was my rent, and I lived-- I lived fine. Anyway, eventually, I met somebody. I got married. And anyway, I worked at different airlines. I ran the biggest travel agency in Beverly Hills, handled all the fancy actors, the Rat Pack. And anyway, so that was that. And I had my daughter, who I adore. And she's in New York, and she's my only real daughter. And eventually, I met-- I went to Velden am Worthersee with my parents, with my daughter. She was three. And I met this guy who was Hungarian, lived in London. And we fell in love, and that was that story. And he lived in London. And eventually, I told my husband what's happening, because I'm such an honest person. And so anyway, George and I, we went-- that's the Rosman name. [PAUSES] Eventually, he came to California. [PAUSES] In Napa, we had a sheepskin factory, very successful. [PAUSES] He had two daughters, who are living here, and I'm very close to them. And traveled the world, had a really good time. And then he got prostate cancer, which was out of the capsule. And three years later, he died. I was 50. And then I figured, I have an apartment-- we had an apartment here at the Lonsdale.

INT: Here?

GR: No, at the Lonsdale in Toronto, because George's daughters lived here. So we went-- we passed here going to Europe all the time. So we figured, instead of go staying at a hotel, we had an apartment at the Lonsdale. So and also, I had lots of friends in LA, so I didn't know where to go. And then I figured, I'll come here. And then, like three years later, I met Steve. I had three Hungarian husbands, but all lived around. And we were married. We were married for 25 years. And he died 11 years ago. He was the healthiest person I ever seen. His father lived to be 106 and mother to Anyway, he got some kind of the worst kind of leukemia from one day to the other. And he died 11 years ago. And so I have four loving stepdaughters, and they all love each other. And one of them, one of my stepdaughters.

INT: They all live here?

GR: Yeah, yeah. All four of them are here, except my daughter is in New York. And I have this one grandson. And the reason I'm doing this is for my grandson to know-- he didn't know my parents. Maybe he knew something about my background, but, you know, he didn't know a hell of a lot, or he doesn't know a hell of a lot. And I figured if one day-- he's 24. If one day he gets married, and he has a family, he should see what grandmother was like. And basically, that's why I'm doing this.

INT: And throughout everything, did you practice any traditional Judaism?

GR: I am a very good Jew. I am. I am not religious. When Steve was alive, we did go to Beth Tzedec, because he and his family went to Beth Tzedec. I know everything because-- I know everything because I had very religious grandparents. And as you can see, I have a really good memory. So I understand. I mean, it was Purim now. And I was telling somebody that my grandfather was-- read the megillah every year. And that was-- that was-- that was a real big thing. So I know, I remember everything. I mean, at one point, George, my second husband, he said, well, he didn't really want to be Jewish because he lost his parents during the Holocaust. And he didn't really want his daughters to be Jewish. Eventually, they both married Jewish people here. And the first Passover, when my father-- my parents were here, it was actually one of the daughters' husbands' parents, he says to me, well, he didn't want to be Jewish. He says, I remember that at Seder, the youngest person reads something. And I said, yeah, that's the Ma Nishtana. And he says, do you think it would be all right-- we had the first grandson-- if I would put Daniel on my knees and I would say it? He says, no. I think that would be really cool. So he says, I don't think I can-- but he was bar mitzvah. But I think he could-- I could read it. I said, I can write it down phonetically, and I did. I mean, I know it to this day, I mean, in my family, everybody had to say the Ma Nishtana. And they said, "Ma Nishtana." [GERMAN]

And everybody had to do the translation.

INT: And during the war years, was there any traditions kept?

GR: Yes, until the Germans came in in March 19, yes. Because my grandparents were alive. Everybody was in their own apartment. My father was away, which was really-- that was the worst thing. Because at times, we didn't know. And there are not too many people who came back from those forced labor camps. And my father, I mean, he went to this with a bunch of people who were athletes, who were really fit. And he wasn't. I mean, he was a good swimmer. He was a good dancer, but he wasn't fit. And he came back. I mean, that was a miracle. It was an absolute miracle. And I have so many friends who lost their fathers. I mean, we went back to school. And, you know, it was really tragic because there were so many-- so many of the girls who lost either mother or father or both. So-- and there's still, I have here, like, two or three. I mean, not too many alive, but a lot of them lost their fathers. So I was-- I'm a lucky person, and I'm a lucky person to be here and be healthy and drive my car and play bridge and do my-- and I have my iPad. So I'm very lucky.

INT: Do you have any other memories that you want to share? Maybe when your father came back or anything that you can remember?

GR: Well, my father came back, that was a big thing. But no. You know, I mean, I have childhood memories, but that has nothing to do with-- nothing to do with it. I mean, I had a really good childhood. I mean, I was-- privileged childhood. And as I said, my mother sent me to this English school, and the whole family said that she is completely meshuga. Because why? Because it was a long walk. And somebody had to walk me and pick me up. But she said-- she said, you should learn another language. So I learned English. And that was very-- my mother was very ambitious. She was very smart. And I think she was ruling the family. [LAUGHING] And she-- basically, my father died in Munich in 1986. And so my mother lived in Munich, and she had friends and everything. But she came every year. She went to Palm Springs with some lady-- nice Hungarian Jewish ladies from New York and from LA. And they had a really, really good time. And-- oh, they had-- I mean, I went down there to visit, and they had parties, and they were playing cards. There was bridge, there was rummy, there was-- Zsa Zsa-- old lady Gabor was around there too. And then she went back to Munich. And then I get this call that my mother is in intensive care. Because I knew-- I knew what was wrong with her. She was coughing on the phone. And she didn't look-- she didn't take her glasses. She took the wrong cough medicine. So she got pneumonia. So she was in the hospital. So I got on the first plane and went to Munich and found her. Anyway, she recouped. And then a few months or maybe a year later, I get this other call that she's in a hospital. And so there I go. I arrived 12 hours late. It's a schlep. And so I go in the next morning, and there she is sitting, the queen. And she says, can you wheel me down to the hairdresser? I can't live with hair like that. So then I made up my mind that this is no life for me. So I came back here, and my sister-in-law, [? Otis ?] has a sister-- lived on Avenue and Russell Hill in this apartment building. And there was this superintendent there who I knew. And at that time, 100 bucks was a lot of money. So I gave whatever her name was 100 bucks. I said, I need an apartment, a two-bedroom apartment with two bathrooms. Oh. She says, you won't believe it, but I just have it. So I rented the apartment. I had all kinds of furniture in Napa because, when I moved, I put it in storage, so I could furnish an apartment. So I called my mother. I said, you know what? I rented an apartment for you. It's five minutes' walk from me, and it's two minutes by car. And then she started saying, I'm not going anywhere. I have a life here, blah, blah, blah. I said, OK. So I put down the phone. And about three or four hours later, the phone rings. And she said, you know, I thought about it. And I think you are right. So she kept her Munich apartment, and she was here, which was-- which was really good, because eventually, I mean, she did this, and she made friends, and she was playing cards. She'd party. But then she got sick here. And she was here close to me. And she's buried here at Beth Tzedec. And then I had to clean this apartment. And I had to clean the Munich apartment, which was-- but that was very amazing because, in Munich, we used to-- we used to correspond by tape with my parents from California, tape recordings. And they kept all the tapes. And I took Monica with me. We took-- and we were sitting all night long, listening to our lives. Because we had-- we told them what was happening every day or every week or something. And she was-- and she spoke-- at that time, she spoke perfect Hungarian because my mother taught her. She didn't live here, but she says, you know, you better learn Hungarian because friends and aunts and uncles are going to come. And they're going to speak Hungarian. And you're going to hate them so much. So she has-- she has a God-given talent for languages. So she can-- she could write-- she could write them. Anyway, so she always spoke Hungarian to them. So we were-- for three nights, we were on the floor till 3 o'clock in the morning at least, and we were listening to our lives. So anyway, that's my life.

INT: Hmm. Wow. And you have some pictures you want to share?

GR: I don't really have-- I mean, I have pictures of my daughter. I don't have-- I mean, this is my picture of my parents.

And this was at a wedding here. That was George Rosman's younger daughter's-- younger daughter wedding here in Toronto at Beth Tzedec. What do you want? Higher?

