GR: That's my father. And he was born in 1904 in Budapest, the youngest of five children. And basically, all the siblings, they said-- they were alive after the war. One of my aunts was in New York, but all the rest of them were alive. Of course, nobody is alive. I mean, I was the youngest grandchild. So that is my-- that is my darling father. Everybody loved him. Doesn't he have a sweet face? So everybody loved him. Anyway, that's my daddy.

And that's my glamorous mom, who was really-- she-- every time I went somewhere, she says, and what are you going to wear? That was one of the most important things. I said, what do you care? I'm just going for coffee. But it was important. Sometimes, I wear jeans, and I look like a bum. And I said, my mother wouldn't like it. And-- but she was brilliant. But this was-- she was 86, 85, 84. I think that was like a year before she died or something. But she was really-- sometimes, she wasn't very pleasant. We all had our problems with her, but she was brilliant. Anyway, that was my mom. And I still think about her. What would my mom say? Or, I should ask my mom. I don't have any pictures of my grandparents.

There are somewhere. I think they are at my daughter's. Because when I emptied out my parents' apartment in Munich, I gave her the family pictures. So my grandfather-- I mean, if it's interesting, he was a very religious person. And as I said, but people came from-- all those rabbis came from everywhere, and they stayed with them. So they must be-- they must have been really religious if they stayed with them. So-- but they were-- mostly my grandmother, who was very smart. If we walked in-- for instance, my mother's sister couldn't go into my grandparents' apartment with a short sleeve, anything. But I walked in one day. I think I was maybe 15, practically naked in a sun dress. And my grandfather said to my grandmother, come, look how beautiful she is. I mean, that was-- family was OK. It was very strange, very strange things. But I learned a lot, and I carried this whole Jewish thing in my mind. And I'm not religious, but I'm a very good Jew. So--

INT: So you still believe in God?

GR: I'm not sure. I am not sure. I'm an agnostic, mostly. I believe that, once we are gone, we are gone. And that's it. Goodbye, Charlie. [PAUSES] The most thing is that, if people remember you. I think nobody really dies [INAUDIBLE] people remember you. I mean, that's my theory.

So my mother's-- my parents are still alive because I'm still talking about them.

