GR: So not religious, not religious.

INT: And what do you think about the anti-Semitism that's happening now?

GR: That is terrible. That is absolutely terrible. And I hate what's happening in Israel. I really hate it. And everybody I talk to hate it. And I am-- I am not a-- I'm not a left-winger. I'm really-- I've voted a lot of times for conservatives or Republicans in my life, not Trump or any of that. But I think what Netanyahu is doing is a terrible shame, and that can ruin Israel in the eyes of the whole world. And it's really very, extremely upsetting, extremely upsetting. So and the anti-Semitism, it's terrible. And it's mostly people like Trump, Orban, and all those right-wingers in the world and in Europe who are really initiating. They let it go. I mean, they don't stand on their feet and say that-- I mean, all those signs in Los Angeles, did you see that sign over the bridge? I mean, yeah, and on Pico Boulevard, when they killed those two guys? I mean, Pico-- I lived in Los Angeles for a number of years, and Pico Boulevard is a wonderful Jewish community there. I mean, there's one little shul after the other and the butcher shops and everything else. And they're really religious. And a little bit over there, there is this beautiful community called something wood-- Beverly Wood, where all the Jewish-- all the modern Orthodox live there. And they have beautiful homes. And can you imagine them going out on the street? I mean, they shop on Pico Boulevard. So, no, I'm very upset, and I love Israel. I've been to Israel several times. And the first time I was in Israel, it was really funny, because it was in 1963. I missed my grandmother with one year. She died in '62. But I had lots of family still there. And I was thinking to myself, oh, my God, I really should know some Ivrit. I went to a Jewish school. But, you know, I knew some words, but I don't. And then I realized that I didn't need my English. I needed my Hungarian because everybody at the Dan Hotel was Hungarian, including the chambermaids. Every cab driver, every hairdresser, and every restaurant, they're all Hungarians. And then the next time I went, then it was Arab because that's when they kicked out the Arabs from the Arab countries-- the Jews from the Arab countries. And then it was the Russians. Then the Russians came. So I had my favorite cousin who was-- he died, but I'm very, very close to his wife and his kids and everything else. And he says to me, you know, all the nice Jewish ladies with the blonde hair, they're all dyeing their hair back to brown because all those Russian women are blonde. [LAUGHING] So anyway, no, I was in Israel last time, maybe five years ago. [PAUSES] And I love every minute of it. And my daughter was there on business in Jerusalem. They had this big meeting, and she was in-- and then she went to Tel Aviv, where my family lives. And they all-- you know, they all love each other. Anyway, that's my story. Anything else you want me to-- or was it an OK interview?

INT: Yeah. If you have any memories or anything you want to share otherwise?

GR: I don't think I have any more. I mean, yeah, it's-- I don't have any desire of going to Hungary. I really don't like them. They were-- I mean, I like-- I like my friends, but the people there, they were worse than the Germans when-- during Hitler. And they were worse than the Russians. And now, they have Orban. I mean, they always sit on the wrong side of the horse. And I have-- I've been-- I've been back several times, I mean, but not for a long time. And that, it wasn't-- when I was back there, there was no anti-Semitism. Or I didn't hear any. Or I didn't hear any. But Budapest-- have you been to Budapest? It's a beautiful city. It's a lovely city. And I went there, because I said, I know the language. I can go to the theater. I even know my way around.

INT: Hmm.

GR: The first-- but the first time I went, it was really funny, because I knew the street names, the old street names. Now, the cab drivers didn't know the old ones. They knew the old ones. So I told them where to go, but I knew exactly. I remembered. And I could go to the theater. We still had some friends alive. But nobody is there anymore. And I don't-- I didn't meet Orban. I meet-- I met him. He was here. And there was this big lunch, and I think it was at the Royal York, if I'm not mistaken. But then he was different. You know, he was on a-- he-- George Soros put him through school. It was a Soros scholarship that Orban went to school on.

